<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=penicillin">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Penicillin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1928">1928</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Penicillin is a β-lactam antibiotic first obtained from Penicillium moulds; it was discovered in 1928 by the Scottish physician Alexander Fleming at St Mary's Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, who saw that mould contaminating a bacterial culture appeared to kill the bacteria. A team led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/howard-florey">Howard Florey</a> and Ernst Chain at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> isolated the purified compound in 1940. Mass-produced in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, it made a major difference to deaths from infected wounds among Allied forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and the three men shared the 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/penicillin.jpg" alt="Penicillin" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author (U.S. National Archives, 514228) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Every_Minute_Lost_on_this_Job_may_Mean..%22_-_NARA_-_514228_(edited).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1928</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>London</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Alexander Fleming · Howard Florey · Ernst Chain · <a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Reports of the antibacterial properties of Penicillium mould go back to the late 19th century, but the article says scientists could not discern what caused the effect and that Fleming was the first to show that Penicillium rubens had antibacterial properties.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/germ-theory-of-disease">Germ theory of disease<span>1658</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=penicillin">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
